  "source_route": [
    "Reuse the existing Fubini-isodiametric projection bound and the actual projection-composition averages to prove the printed beta-weighted degree-drop inequality.",
    "Reuse the original Cartesian intrinsic-volume convolution of 3.2.36 and the actual elementary symmetric cube coefficients. A finite degree constant gives the printed cube-product intrinsic estimate, with zero side and degree endpoints.",
    "Use the existing opaque equal-cube subdivision and metric Caratheodory covering laws. Choose the first mesh and a smaller second-factor cutoff, then remove the positive polynomial margin. The actual original Q_1 cube-product upper estimate retains arbitrary second factors of finite mass.",
    "Reuse restriction and image measures, Borel preimages and the all-set isometric-embedding theorem. The actual product slice is a measure with measurable Borel sets, finite compact values and translation invariance.",
    "Reuse Borel regularisation, the countable compact Euclidean cover and Haar uniqueness. Include the zero-measure branch. The original unit-cube upper bound controls the finite nonnegative Haar multiplier; domination by regularisation gives the arbitrary first-factor upper bound.",
    "For infinite second-factor mass, the original ordinary partial-product domain forces positive first-factor mass and an infinite right member. No undefined ordinary product is silently assigned a value."
  ],
  "coverage_qualification": "This closes the original product upper inequality in 3.2.45, including arbitrary factors, both zero-degree endpoints and infinite second-factor mass whenever the original product is defined. The finite-second-mass helper also proves a total-product zero-factor extension. The full mixed-volume section comparison, sharp Q_infinity <= Q_1 and Gillespie product lower/equality assertions remain pending. Exponent continuity is already complete. Preserve the printed-estimate counterexamples and explicit user omissions.",
